// @check
// @error-at 38:0 no field 'b' on {a: boolean}
// THE DONE-SET'S KEY IS THE ASSERTION, and the message text is what carries it.
//
// A redeclared type name overwrites `cUserTypes` in pass 0a, so BOTH declarations
// resolve into the LAST placeholder and the fill registry is what stops the second
// body being appended to the first's. The registry is keyed by ARENA INDEX, so the
// two declarations below name ONE arena entry, it fills exactly once, and `Redecl`
// denotes ONE declaration's shape — never the merge of both.
//
// `{a: boolean}` in the expected message is the whole pin, in two halves:
//
//   `b` ABSENT  — the second declaration did not also fill the shared placeholder.
//     A registry keyed by DECLARATION STATEMENT instead (the looser key: two
//     statements, so two fills) merges the bodies into `{a: boolean, b: string}`,
//     and this file's verdict moves to an argument-mismatch at the `use({ a: true })`
//     call instead. Same for a registry that answers "not yet filled" always.
//
//   `boolean`, not `Flag` and not a one-member union WRAPPER — `Flag` is declared
//     BELOW the declaration that names it, so resolving `Redecl`'s field has to
//     demand `Flag`'s fill out of source order (`forceDeclFill`) and
//     `declaredTyOfName`'s transparency collapse has to find its one member present.
//     An unfilled `Flag` renders as the wrapper here.
//
// Both halves have to hold at once, which is why they share one file: making the fill
// order-independent is what introduced the second caller of `fillTypeDeclAt`, and the
// done-set is the only thing standing between that second caller and a double fill.
//
// The corpus cannot reach this on its own — no other case under tests/cases/ declares
// one type name twice, so every alternative key above is byte-identical across all of
// it and invisible without this file.

type Redecl = { a: Flag }
type Redecl = { b: string }
type Flag = boolean

function use(r: Redecl) {
  print(r.a)
  print(r.b)
}

use({ a: true })
